The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
err = mvpp2_rx_refill(port, bm_pool, pp, pool);
if (err) {
    netdev_err(port->dev, "failed to refill BM pools\n");
    goto err_drop_frame;
}
```
(These lines appeared after `mvpp2_run_xdp` and after `build_skb`/`slab_build_skb`.)
```c
err_drop_frame:
    dev->stats.rx_errors++;
    mvpp2_rx_error(port, rx_desc);
    /* Return the buffer to the pool */
    if (rx_status & MVPP2_RXD_BUF_HDR)
        mvpp2_buff_hdr_pool_put(port, rx_desc, pool, rx_status);
    else
        mvpp2_bm_pool_put(port, pool, dma_addr, phys_addr);
```
Reason:
The refill was performed *after* the current buffer had already been handed to XDP (via `mvpp2_run_xdp`) or attached to an skb. When the refill failed, the error path unconditionally returned the buffer to the hardware BM pool using `mvpp2_bm_pool_put`. However, at that point the buffer’s memory may already have been recycled, queued for XDP_TX, or freed by skb free, causing hardware DMA into freed memory.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
+		err = mvpp2_rx_refill(port, bm_pool, pp, pool);
+		if (err) {
+			netdev_err(port->dev, "failed to refill BM pools\n");
+			goto err_drop_frame;
+		}
```
(Inserted before the XDP and skb processing blocks.)
```c
 		if (!skb) {
 			netdev_warn(port->dev, "skb build failed\n");
-			goto err_drop_frame;
+			if (pp) {
+				page_pool_put_page(pp, virt_to_head_page(data),
+						   rx_bytes + MVPP2_MH_SIZE,
+						   true);
+			} else {
+				dma_unmap_single_attrs(dev->dev.parent, dma_addr,
+						       bm_pool->buf_size,
+						       DMA_FROM_DEVICE,
+						       DMA_ATTR_SKIP_CPU_SYNC);
+				mvpp2_frag_free(bm_pool, pp, data);
+			}
+			goto err_drop_frame_retired;
 		}
```
```c
+err_drop_frame_retired:
+		dev->stats.rx_errors++;
+		mvpp2_rx_error(port, rx_desc);
```
Reason:
The refill is moved *before* the buffer is passed to XDP or the skb. If refill fails now, the buffer is still owned by the driver, so the existing `err_drop_frame` path (which returns the buffer to BM) is safe. For the case where refill succeeded but skb allocation fails, the buffer is no longer owned by the driver (it was attached to an skb attempt), so the code explicitly frees it via `page_pool_put_page` or DMA unmapping + free, and jumps to `err_drop_frame_retired` which only records the error without returning the buffer to BM. This preserves the BM pool depth and prevents use-after-free.

